INT. GENERATION SHIP "WILLOW" - HYDROPONICS BAY - SHIP NIGHT

Terraced troughs of lettuce under violet grow-lamps. Condensation. A gentle hum. Through a long viewport, the stars do not move.

AUGUSTINE WRAY (70s, caretaker, thin, patient) works along a trough with a pair of tweezers, removing a yellowed leaf. Behind him, a young woman, TAMSIN ODEH-VARGA (20s, apprentice, coveralls tied around her waist) tends a seedling tray.

AUGUSTINE
Pinch it where it meets the stem. Not above.

TAMSIN
I know.

AUGUSTINE
Then why are you above?

TAMSIN
I'm not above.

He glances at her tray. She is above. She moves her fingers down an inch. He nods and returns to his row.

TAMSIN (CONT'D)
(muttering)
Every day the same leaf.

AUGUSTINE
Every day a different leaf.

TAMSIN
It looks the same.

AUGUSTINE
That's because you aren't looking.

She sighs. Wipes her hands on her coveralls. Looks at the stars.

TAMSIN
Two hundred and six years.

AUGUSTINE
And fourteen days.

TAMSIN
My great-grandmother's great-grandmother boarded this ship.

AUGUSTINE
And never saw the end of it.

TAMSIN
Neither will I.

AUGUSTINE
No.

TAMSIN
Neither will you.

AUGUSTINE
Not at my age, no.

They work in silence. A drip falls from the irrigation line. Plink. Tamsin counts the drips on her fingers.

TAMSIN
(to the room)
Does anyone else feel strange down here?

AUGUSTINE
Anyone else would be a crowd.

TAMSIN
I mean the whole ship.

AUGUSTINE
Only the ones who are paying attention.

She laughs, short. He allows himself a small smile and prunes another leaf.

TAMSIN
Augustine?

AUGUSTINE
Yes.

TAMSIN
What do you do when the lettuce is done?

AUGUSTINE
We plant more lettuce.

TAMSIN
No. When it is all done. When the ship arrives.

AUGUSTINE
Ah.

He straightens, one hand on the small of his back. He looks at her for a while.

AUGUSTINE (CONT'D)
Then somebody takes a seed and puts it in real ground.

TAMSIN
And the lettuce will think it's dying.

AUGUSTINE
It will think it's being born.

Tamsin picks up the tray. Carries it to the next trough. The violet light moves over her face as she passes under each lamp.

TAMSIN
(softly)
I hope I'm in the right row, then.

AUGUSTINE
You are.

TAMSIN ^
You always say that.

AUGUSTINE
You always doubt it.

TAMSIN
It's my job to doubt.

AUGUSTINE
It is mine to be right.

She sets the seedlings in their slots, firm, careful, pressing each stem upright with a fingertip. Augustine watches. When she finishes, he picks up the watering can and hands it to her.

AUGUSTINE (CONT'D)
Go on. Slowly.

She pours. The water hisses on dark soil. A faint green smell rises.

TAMSIN
It's quiet tonight.

AUGUSTINE
It is always quiet.

TAMSIN
Tonight it is different.

AUGUSTINE
Then listen.

They stand still, side by side, hands at their sides, and for a long moment there is only the sound of something, very small, growing.
